  "messages": [
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Choose one next planning step using the local decision below. Return exactly one listed statement and stop. No other planning choices have been supplied.\n{\"context\": \"A separate waiting area has brief activities that can be stopped immediately when a guest’s next game becomes available.\", \"decision\": \"How should guests use short activities while waiting?\", \"goal\": \"Help guests find compatible games, understand how to play, and enjoy the evening without avoidable waiting or confusion.\", \"problem\": \"A community room is hosting an evening of board games for adults with different levels of experience. Several tables, a host, willing game teachers, short waiting activities, and a varied collection are available. Guests can decline any game and join a later one. Every game has an agreed latest stopping time before the room closes, and ordinary refreshments and conversation have their own small areas.\"}\nCANDIDATE STATEMENTS:\nGuests choose short activities freely from the waiting-area basket and return them when called to a game. Waiting remains flexible because each guest can stop without delaying other people or losing a promised table place.\nThe host directs newly waiting guests toward an existing brief activity and tells its participants when another game is ready. Small waiting groups remain socially connected without needing to monitor table availability themselves.\nA volunteer offers a brief shared activity to waiting guests and checks the next-game list between small rounds. People have an easy way to meet while the volunteer keeps the activity responsive to upcoming openings."
    }
  ],
